Overview:
Incident classification is the process of identifying and categorizing security events based on their nature, attack type, and impact. Proper classification helps Security Operations Center (SOC) analysts organize incidents, prioritize investigations, and apply appropriate response procedures. Standardized classification also improves communication between security teams and ensures consistent documentation across incidents.
Incident Categories:
Security incidents are classified according to the type of threat or attack involved. Some common incident categories include:
Incident Type
Description
Example
Malware
Malicious software designed to disrupt systems or steal data
Trojan detected on a workstation
Phishing
Fraudulent emails or messages used to steal credentials or install malware
Fake login page sent via email
DDoS (Distributed Denial of Service)
Attack that overwhelms systems or networks with traffic
Website outage due to massive botnet traffic
Insider Threat
Malicious or negligent activity from internal users
Employee exporting sensitive company data
Data Exfiltration
Unauthorized transfer of data outside the organization
Large volume of confidential files sent to external IP
Correct classification allows SOC analysts to apply the appropriate investigation and response strategy.
Incident Classification Frameworks:
To ensure consistent labeling and analysis, SOC teams rely on standardized security frameworks.
MITRE ATT&CK Framework:
The MITRE Corporation developed the MITRE ATT&CK framework to document real-world adversary behavior.
It categorizes attacks into tactics (attacker goals) and techniques (methods used).
Example:
Tactic: Initial Access
Technique: Phishing – T1566
This mapping helps analysts understand how attackers gain entry and move through a network.
ENISA Incident Taxonomy:
The European Union Agency for Cybersecurity provides a structured taxonomy for classifying cybersecurity incidents across organizations. It standardizes categories such as
Malware infections
Network attacks
Data breaches
Identity theft
This taxonomy improves communication between incident response teams and regulatory bodies.
VERIS Framework:
The VERIS framework is used to document security incidents in a structured format.
VERIS focuses on four core elements:
Actor – Who performed the attack.
Action – What actions were taken.
Asset – Which system was targeted.
Attribute – Impact on confidentiality, integrity, or availability.
This framework is widely used for threat intelligence and breach reporting.
Contextual Metadata in Incident Analysis:
In addition to classification, SOC analysts enrich incidents with contextual metadata to support investigations. Important metadata includes:
Affected systems (servers, endpoints, databases).
Timestamp of the incident.
Source and destination IP addresses.
Indicators of Compromise (IOCs) such as malicious file hashes or domains.
User accounts involved in the incident.
This information helps analysts correlate alerts and identify the root cause of the attack.
Learning Outcome:
Through the study of incident classification methods, the intern developed the ability to:
Categorize security incidents based on attack type.
Map incidents to standardized frameworks such as MITRE ATT&CK.
Enrich incidents with contextual metadata for effective investigation.
Support structured documentation and reporting within SOC operations.
